# Architecture Patterns & Development Workflow

## Core Architecture Overview

### Flake-Based Structure
```
flake.nix (Entry Point)
├── inputs: nixpkgs, phoenix, custom packages
├── outputs: nixosConfigurations.example
└── modules: configuration.nix + hardware + custom modules
```

### Hierarchical Module System
```
configuration.nix (Main Config)
├── hardware-configuration.nix
├── modules/dotfiles-manager.nix (Core Infrastructure)
├── modules/applications.nix (Module Registry)
└── modules/*/default.nix (Individual Modules)
```

### Module Categories
- **Applications**: User-facing programs (zed, git, fish)
- **Services**: System services (adguardhome, unbound)
- **Desktop Environment**: UI components (hyprland, waybar)
- **Games**: Gaming applications (steam, gamemode)
- **Development**: Dev tools (nix-dev, direnv)

## Development Workflow Patterns

### 1. Adding a New Application

**Step 1: Create Module Structure**
```bash
mkdir -p modules/<app>
touch modules/<app>/default.nix
```

**Step 2: Implement Basic Module**
```nix
# modules/<app>/default.nix
{ config, pkgs, lib, ... }:

with lib;

let cfg = config.applications.<app>;
in {
  options.applications.<app> = {
    enable = mkEnableOption "<App description>";
  };

  config = mkIf cfg.enable {
    environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [ <app> ];
  };
}
```

**Step 3: Register Module**
```nix
# modules/applications.nix
imports = [
  # ... existing imports
  ./<app>
];
```

**Step 4: Enable Application**
```nix
# configuration.nix
applications = {
  # ... existing apps
  <app>.enable = true;
};
```

**Step 5: Test Deployment**
- Ask the user to build or switch the system using the standard delegation template.
- You may suggest `nixos-rebuild dry-run --flake .#example` (no sudo) or `nix build --no-link .#nixosConfigurations.example.config.system.build.toplevel --no-update-lock-file --no-write-lock-file` when a read-only check is needed.

### 2. Adding Configuration Management

**Only do this if explicitly requested!**

**Step 1: Add Config Files**
```bash
# Create native format config files
touch modules/<app>/settings.json
touch modules/<app>/config.toml
```

**Step 2: Update Module with Dotfiles**
```nix
# modules/<app>/default.nix
let cfg = config.applications.<app>;
in {
  options.applications.<app> = {
    enable = mkEnableOption "<App description>";

    dotfiles = {
      enable = mkOption {
        type = types.bool;
        default = cfg.enable;
        description = "Manage <app> dotfiles";
      };
    };
  };

  config = mkIf cfg.enable {
    environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [ <app> ];

    dotfiles = mkIf cfg.dotfiles.enable {
      enable = true;
      modules.<app> = {
        enable = true;
        mappings = {
          settings = {
            source = "settings.json";
            target = "$HOME/.config/<app>/settings.json";
          };
          config = {
            source = "config.toml";
            target = "$HOME/.config/<app>/config.toml";
          };
        };
      };
    };
  };
}
```

### 3. Adding a Service Module

**Pattern for System Services**
```nix
# modules/<service>/default.nix
{ config, lib, ... }:

with lib;

let cfg = config.applications.<service>;
in {
  options.applications.<service> = {
    enable = mkEnableOption "<Service description>";
  };

  config = mkIf cfg.enable {
    services.<service> = {
      enable = true;
      # Service-specific configuration
      port = 8080;
      settings = {
        # Configuration options
      };
    };
  };
}
```

### 4. Adding Desktop Environment Components

**Pattern for Complex UI Modules**
```nix
# modules/desktop-environment/<component>/default.nix
{ config, pkgs, lib, ... }:

with lib;

let cfg = config.applications.<component>;
in {
  options.applications.<component> = {
    enable = mkEnableOption "<Component description>";
  };

  config = mkIf cfg.enable {
    # Core component
    programs.<component>.enable = true;

    # Supporting packages
    environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [
      # Related packages
    ];

    # Environment setup
    environment.sessionVariables = {
      # Required variables
    };

    # System integration
    security.polkit.enable = true;
    services.<service>.enable = true;

    # Optional: Display manager
    services.greetd.settings.default_session.command = "...";
  };
}
```

## Module Interaction Patterns

### Service Integration
```nix
# modules/adguardhome/default.nix
services.adguardhome = {
  enable = true;
  settings = {
    dns = {
      upstream_dns = [
        "127.0.0.1:${toString config.services.unbound.port}"
      ];
    };
  };
};
```

### Conditional Dependencies
```nix
# Only enable if another service is enabled
config = mkIf (cfg.enable && config.services.otherService.enable) {
  # Additional configuration
};
```

### Cross-Module References
```nix
# Reference another module's configuration
let
  otherPort = config.services.otherService.port;
in {
  services.currentService = {
    port = otherPort + 1;  # Use port after other service
  };
};
```

## Configuration Management Patterns

### Environment Variables
```nix
environment.sessionVariables = {
  # Global variables
  EDITOR = "zed";

  # Hardware-specific
  __GLX_VENDOR_LIBRARY_NAME = lib.mkIf cfg.nvidiaSupport "nvidia";

  # Application-specific
  ZED_ALLOW_EMULATED_GPU = "0";
};
```

### System Packages
```nix
environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [
  # Core application
  application

  # Supporting tools
  supporting-tool1
  supporting-tool2

  # Conditional packages
] ++ lib.optionals cfg.extraTools [
  extra-tool1
  extra-tool2
];
```

### User Configuration
```nix
users.users.alice = {
  shell = lib.mkIf cfg.enableShell pkgs.fish;
  extraGroups = [ "group1" "group2" ];
};
```

## Testing & Validation Patterns

### Module Testing Checklist
- [ ] Module builds without errors
- [ ] Application installs correctly
- [ ] Configuration files are created
- [ ] Symlinks point to correct locations
- [ ] Application starts and functions
- [ ] User can modify configuration

### Debugging Commands
```bash
# Check module syntax
nix-instantiate --parse modules/<app>/default.nix

# Dry run rebuild (no sudo, read-only)
nixos-rebuild dry-run --flake .#example

# Build system closure without switching
nix build --no-link .#nixosConfigurations.example.config.system.build.toplevel --no-update-lock-file --no-write-lock-file
```

### Common Issues & Solutions

**Issue**: Module not found
**Solution**: Check `modules/applications.nix` imports

**Issue**: Configuration not applied
**Solution**: Verify `configuration.nix` has `applications.<app>.enable = true`

**Issue**: Symlinks not created
**Solution**: Check dotfiles module is enabled and mappings are correct

**Issue**: Service not starting
**Solution**: Check service configuration and dependencies

## Best Practices

### Code Organization
- Keep modules focused on single responsibility
- Use descriptive names and comments
- Follow established patterns
- Test changes incrementally

### Configuration Management
- Start with minimal configuration
- Add complexity only when needed
- Document configuration options
- Version control all changes

### Maintenance
- Regularly update flake inputs
- Test rebuilds after changes
- Keep documentation current
- Review and clean up unused modules

### Collaboration
- Use clear commit messages
- Document breaking changes
- Review module additions
- Share configuration patterns
